.TH APERI 1 2026-11-15 "aperi @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aperi \- open a file, directory or URI with the associated program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aperi
.I file
|
.I directory
|
.I uri
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aperi
is a simple resource opener.
It reads its configuration file, finds the first rule matching its argument,
based on the file extension, the URI schema or the argument type, and
replaces itself with the associated program, passing it the argument.
.PP
Without arguments,
.B aperi
prints its version and a short usage message.
Arguments after the first one are ignored.
.SS Argument handling
If the argument starts with
.BR file:// ,
that prefix is removed and the rest is percent decoded.
.PP
If the argument is an existing file or directory, it is passed to the
program as an absolute path.
Otherwise, if it contains
.BR :// ,
it is handled as a URI and passed unchanged.
Otherwise
.B aperi
exits with an error.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file contains one rule per line.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
Every other line has the form:
.PP
.RS
.IR pattern [ ,\c
.IR pattern ]...\c
.BR = [ % ]\c
.I program
.RI [ arg ]...
.RE
.PP
Lines are checked in order and the first one with a matching
.I pattern
is used: put more specific rules before more generic ones.
If its program can't be executed, an error is printed and the search
continues with the following lines.
If no rule matches, nothing is launched.
.SS Patterns
.TP
.I ext
Matches a file whose name ends with
.BI . ext\c
, compared case insensitively, e.g.\&
.B pdf
or
.BR tar.gz .
.TP
.IB schema ://\c
.RI [ prefix ]
A pattern containing
.B ://
matches a URI starting with it, compared case sensitively, e.g.\&
.B https://
or
.BR https://youtu.be/ .
.TP
.B /
Matches a directory.
.TP
.B /*
Matches any argument.
It is usually the last rule, as a catch all.
.SS Program and arguments
.I program
is the name of an executable, searched in
.BR PATH ,
or its path.
It is executed with the
.IR arg s
given in the rule, followed by the
.B aperi
argument.
.PP
If the
.B =
is followed by
.BR % ,
the
.B aperi
argument is not appended and these placeholders are expanded in the
.IR arg s:
.TP
.B %f
the
.B aperi
argument (absolute path or URI);
.TP
.B %%
a literal
.BR % .
.PP
Other placeholders are invalid and give unspecified results.
.SS Quoting
Patterns containing
.B ,
or
.BR = ,
arguments containing spaces and programs starting with
.B %
must be enclosed in double quotes.
Two consecutive double quotes inside a quoted string produce a literal
double quote.
.SH WRAPPERS
Before reading the configuration file, for a file argument
.B aperi
looks in the
.I wrappers
subdirectory of the configuration directory for an executable named as the
file extension.
For multiple extensions the longest one is tried first: for
.I archive.tar.gz
the wrapper
.I tar.gz
is tried before
.IR gz .
.PP
A wrapper is executed with the absolute path of the file as its only
argument and takes precedence over the configuration file, which is used
if no wrapper can be executed.
Wrappers handle cases that rules can't express, such as passing the
argument in a special way.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/aperi/config
The user configuration file
.RI ( ~/.config/aperi/config
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set).
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/aperi/wrappers/
The user wrappers directory.
.TP
.I @SYSCONFDIR@/aperi/
The global configuration directory, with the same layout.
It is used only if the user configuration directory doesn't exist.
.TP
.I @DATADIR@/doc/aperi/examples/config
An example configuration file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the user configuration.
.TP
.B HOME
Used when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to find the programs to execute.
.SH EXIT STATUS
When a program is launched,
.B aperi
is replaced by it and the exit status is the program's.
.PP
Otherwise
.B aperi
exits with status 1 if the argument is neither an existing file or
directory nor a URI, or if a matching wrapper or program couldn't be
executed, and with status 0 if no rule matches.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# Videos and pdf files
mp4,mkv=mpv
pdf=zathura

# More specific URI rules first
https://youtu.be/=mpv
http://,https://=firefox

# Directories in a terminal file manager
/=%foot yazi %f

# Quoting and placeholders: echo receives a single argument
txt=%echo "The file ""%f"" costs 5%%"

# Ask for everything else
/*=app-chooser
.EE
.PP
To use
.B aperi
in place of
.BR xdg-open (1),
put a link to it named
.B xdg-open
in a directory that comes first in
.BR PATH .
.BR aperi-setup (1)
configures a user account, including the default applications and the
.B xdg-open
link.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR app-chooser (1),
.BR aperi-session (1),
.BR aperi-setup (1),
.BR xdg-open (1)
.PP
.I @DATADIR@/doc/aperi/README.md
.SH AUTHORS
Matteo Beniamino <m.beniamino@tautologica.org>
